[681.65, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ **Step 5 report: Range**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Did.** The plan now has product principles (§1b) that make it fair, safe and private by default. Five of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the new or changed tickets enforce them. I borrowed from four fields. I ran the attack and quest simulations\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  myself, so I used no subagents. The plan checker passes with 36 tickets, and I snapshotted the plan to\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `.ambition/steps/5/`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Field | What I borrowed | Edge taken |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Lead mobile engineer | A walking skeleton first, four milestones that are each a ride-able build, a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  definition of done | Four milestones (M1 to M4) in §8 |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Behavioural game designer | Simulate each rider type before fixing the reward design | Personalised weekly\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  quests |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Road-safety human-factors engineer | Hazard analysis of the incentives themselves | No rewards for speed\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  or risk, and nothing shown while moving |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Privacy attacker (red team) | Attack the published data, and look for who could be harmed | Private by\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  default, with honest privacy-zone copy |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What the red team found.**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **The plan's privacy zone was broken.** A fixed-centre, fixed-radius zone gave up the home to 0.0 m error\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  from 3 published rides. A secret offset plus radius jitter helps, but a second attack (extending each\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  track's heading backwards) still finds home within about 17 to 61 m after 30 rides. A 400 m zone raises that\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  to 55 to 175 m. Hiding geometry can't be the main protection.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **So visibility is now approval-gated.** Follows need the rider's approval, maps go only to approved\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  followers, and there is no public discovery or live location. The shipped zone settings are untested against\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the circle-fit attack, so ticket T-022 requires that run.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Fixed quests are unfair.** \"Climb 800 m\" is completed 0% of weeks by commuters and 100% by keen riders.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Personalised targets give every rider type 35 to 46%.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Missing requirement.** Apple guideline 1.2 requires report, block and contact info, so I added ticket\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  T-045. Effort is now 130 dev-days, about 12.4 weeks for 3 developers at 70% focus.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Not resolved.**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The minimum age and children's-privacy rules for sharing location need legal advice. Apple's page sets no\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  general minimum age.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The simulations use invented rider types, so real ride data should replace them.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - I didn't verify the road-safety field's claims from external sources. The principles come from design\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  reasoning, not citations.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
